  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Medium range guidance are beginning to converge\non a solution for the mid to late-week period, but there are \nstill some notable differences. Where guidance is fairly well \nclustered is with an amplified upper trough that will be \nshifting east out of the Central U.S. While there is still some \npotential for a cutoff low to develop, most guidance have now \ntrended towards more of a progressive and open wave as it moves \nthrough the Eastern U.S. Guidance differ on the timing, which \nleads to differences in precipitation placement, temperatures, \netc. But, most guidance is now centering on the best chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms coming Thursday and/or Friday. Of \nnote, some guidance are showing anomalous moisture and higher- \nthan-normal instability for early November. This would support \nthe potential of some higher rainfall rates and totals should \nthis materialize. It may even support at least a marginal risk \nof strong to severe thunderstorms. While there is some synoptic \nsupport for this potential, machine learning guidance doesn't \nshow that strong of a signal. It's something worth keeping an \neye on as the evolution of the upper level, and surface, pattern\nbecome more clear.\n\nThe other aspect of the forecast that appears to be coming more\nclear this week is the potential for above to well above normal\ntemperatures, especially Wednesday and Thursday. While record \nhighs may not be broken, warming low-level thicknesses in \nadvance of the approaching trough should support highs warming \ninto the 70s, with some low 80s possible inland. Meanwhile, \novernight lows are forecast to be closer to our normal highs for\nthis time of year. Some record warm lows may be in jeopardy. \nFor reference, normal highs are around 70 in early November, \nwith normal lows in the 40s inland and 50s along the coast.",
